#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068455 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "a316177e-e646-576c-bcab-d6cb0a450a03" :question/math-academy-id "e-300" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\rightarrow 3}\\dfrac{x^3-27}{x-3}$." :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=3$ gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {x^3-27}{x-3} &= \\lim_{x\\to3} \\dfrac {[x^3-27]'}{[x-3]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 3} \\dfrac {3x^2}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 3(3)^2 \\\\[5pt] &= 27 \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068456 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "95c88e72-86e2-5627-b121-beafc7986d76" :question/math-academy-id "e-301" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\, \\frac {\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nso we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac {\\left[\\sin^2 (2x)\\right]'}{[x^2]'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2\\cdot \\sin (2x) \\cdot\\cos (2x)\\cdot 2}{2x} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0}{2\\cos (2x)} \\cdot \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\cos(2\\cdot 0)\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nBut that's okay, because we can just apply L'Hopital's rule again! We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac{\\sin^2 (2x)}{x^2} &= 2\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\dfrac{\\sin (2x)}{x} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {[\\sin (2x)]'}{[x]'} \\\\[5pt] &= 2\\lim_{x\\to0} \\dfrac {2\\cos (2x)}{1} \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\cos(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ &=4. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "b9a1c3de-68cb-5e07-a71b-41f9ac77c832" :question/math-academy-id "e-302" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)}$." :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\ln\\left(x^2\\right) = -\\infty\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2}{x^2} = \\infty\n$$\n\nrespectively,\nso we have the case\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{-\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\n.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)} &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{[\\ln\\left(x^2\\right)]'}{\\left(\\dfrac{2}{x^2}\\right)'} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{\\left(1/x^2\\right)\\cdot 2x}{-\\left({4}/{x^3}\\right)} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{x^{-2} \\cdot 2x}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\lim_{x\\to 0} \\dfrac{2x^{-1}}{-4x^{-3}} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\lim_{x\\to 0} {x^2} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\[5pt] &= 0. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "8e96079a-3933-5562-a4f5-ddd87ac5d05f" :question/math-academy-id "e-303" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac {x^6}{5x^6-4}}$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\infty}{\\infty}\n$$\n\nso applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {x^6}{5x^6-4} &= \\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\dfrac {[x^6]'}{[5x^6-4]'} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {6x^5}{5(6x^5)-0} \\\\ &= \\lim_{x\\to \\infty} \\dfrac {1}{5} \\\\ &= \\dfrac{1}{5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2d10e48-b44e-563c-aa37-d0dd37331a74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264165} {:answer/id #uuid "58426629-e3d7-5044-a7da-44aa577ceb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264166} {:answer/id #uuid "92f5bc3a-658c-564e-acaf-bc05f92a51cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264167} {:answer/id #uuid "73ade856-4ee3-526d-9f01-2d97b054d999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264168} {:answer/id #uuid "e05cbe87-e43b-51d4-bfe6-1eea9d5b0dbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73ade856-4ee3-526d-9f01-2d97b054d999" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264168} :answer-field/id #uuid "17ee673c-b7d2-578a-8bca-cc6d3c5f397f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264164}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c771f81-c5b8-51aa-b04a-9b9d2e6048b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-114239" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{-x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{2}]^{′}}{{[{e}^{-x}]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{-x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{-x}} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{[2x]^{′}}{[{e}^{-x}]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{2}{-{e}^{-x}} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{x} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264170 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2588752-2571-5af5-8f1c-40164f54d92f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264172} {:answer/id #uuid "f4eea8a1-99b2-5df2-8aa9-4b7be41bed25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264173} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ea63fd-2ec6-533e-bc77-e0ee6d142100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264174} {:answer/id #uuid "44381b3f-6776-56a0-9b39-7caf8acc8342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264175} {:answer/id #uuid "bb598ad9-346b-57fd-86bc-464fad453a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb598ad9-346b-57fd-86bc-464fad453a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264176} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbf03e82-4adc-5a2b-a301-257c99585aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264171}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0910a0b7-374a-578c-b2a2-b95c0ef0d221" :question/math-academy-id "q-114250" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1}=$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=1$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{-2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{2x-2} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{{x}^{2}-2x+1} & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)}{x-1} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)]^{′}}{[x-1]^{′}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)}{1} \\\\  & =-{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d858df7-bf48-5787-a254-25eef37bd904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264179} {:answer/id #uuid "3635b4b7-dbc1-50e2-9508-07653c5eae0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264180} {:answer/id #uuid "449e0cd6-3877-5ca5-8136-29257943bc39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264181} {:answer/id #uuid "a691ab8a-ab38-57ad-b326-c9142195c3c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264182} {:answer/id #uuid "bab86782-d9bb-56c7-bad8-4ede4c44f9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab86782-d9bb-56c7-bad8-4ede4c44f9b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264183} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfb649e-94a5-5d22-bd25-8a9d0fc9807b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264178}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7704ba1b-0c04-5988-a47b-f50e803b20fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-115518" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}){cos}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {\\frac{\\pi }{2}}^{-}}{sin}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264184 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b125944-1856-5ab1-beb7-434241d41c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264186} {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} {:answer/id #uuid "2c451fa2-fc51-5189-9cb0-6f4c159f8621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264188} {:answer/id #uuid "619df33a-7843-5c74-9da5-34186caf63b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264189} {:answer/id #uuid "bc33fa1a-f686-5b11-9603-3f8b71680708" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264190}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b136a36e-4ad7-53f5-9af2-e8d0cdf01a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264187} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ad13d99-b787-5977-a513-f2b4171b3171" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264185}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1f71e509-23a7-5349-9c54-3e2d5f1db3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31257" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\sqrt{x}+4x+3}{2x+3} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\sqrt{x}+4x+3]^{′}}{[2x+3]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}+4)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb36c53-2c0c-5aad-a5a4-9ec26dc9c4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bb36c53-2c0c-5aad-a5a4-9ec26dc9c4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264193} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc4e6e79-8887-584d-9683-5e1c83d5229e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264192}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e87652e-4354-5168-9826-2b467c5af2d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-314154" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=8$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-16x+64]}^{′}}{[18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{x}^{2}-16x+64}{18\\sqrt{x+1}-3x-30} & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2x-16}{9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{{[2x-16]}^{′}}{{[9(x+1)^{-1/2}-3]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 8}\\frac{2}{-\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}{lim}_{x\\to 8}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}(8+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295a7726-c40a-5634-b4bd-f9153064e185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264196} {:answer/id #uuid "070a9933-e030-5e90-91ee-ba93985c8446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264197} {:answer/id #uuid "96a4b77a-1bf6-5734-8a77-5f373a0b0900" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264198} {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} {:answer/id #uuid "83eb24de-5359-5e54-a584-738f4806185e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4223010a-24af-52c6-8552-a359db7cafc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264199} :answer-field/id #uuid "21e33387-f9f3-5179-b023-40bc792c4b37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264195}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba7f82da-4dd1-526e-9dfc-73ae8a9ea727" :question/math-academy-id "q-31517" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}=$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1]^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cd9f306-d637-5ca3-8d1d-0c1d2aec3aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066546} {:answer/id #uuid "ade54fef-10b8-5fce-a63a-0a3210d034e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066547} {:answer/id #uuid "0c103329-e81d-5fa1-a41a-eb77cecfef28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066548} {:answer/id #uuid "26f9c8fb-12f0-5d9b-a1ff-c25a8359ec2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066549} {:answer/id #uuid "689f3567-e3f9-5229-8450-666fb40527d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cd9f306-d637-5ca3-8d1d-0c1d2aec3aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066546} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8a725b-c7f8-5b3a-b6a2-1034bd04bc47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066545}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05d2d2b4-376a-594e-a55c-c9c3393ae7ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-31596" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x \\to 0}\\frac{x\\cos x}{\\ln (x + 1)}$?" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(\\frac{1}{x+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{(\\frac{1}{0+1})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264201 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91acd08-7285-5673-917a-8d232c5247b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264203} {:answer/id #uuid "c36e99e3-ed7c-5d00-8577-227a854bfd94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264204} {:answer/id #uuid "da3b71ca-1326-51aa-8241-88b0e8a73db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264205} {:answer/id #uuid "be8889d9-c67e-5fd1-ab14-0e39d45ae81f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264206} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba81de5-6077-5f2c-b51e-759ffd25aab9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da3b71ca-1326-51aa-8241-88b0e8a73db5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264205} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15c4033-23b7-5082-a9c1-42aa925529a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264202}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b111c6b4-8251-5718-bcbd-4e62b98ac06d" :question/math-academy-id "q-31599" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡0-{sin}^{2}⁡0}{2(0)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d21a4d4d-4412-53e5-96a0-1ef04336a400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264210} {:answer/id #uuid "d676868b-83f4-55df-8f50-ba41a77b1a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264211} {:answer/id #uuid "82aeadd7-7668-5325-aee1-07791f347f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264212} {:answer/id #uuid "81f5109d-29b2-5253-a2f9-50b42c840bb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264213} {:answer/id #uuid "219bc263-5c1a-5386-a7b2-76e8060b9e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "219bc263-5c1a-5386-a7b2-76e8060b9e1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264214} :answer-field/id #uuid "239fc2e8-f568-502a-a262-5043da11f00a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264209}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b71d8ef8-8478-5971-9e1b-9300d94d190a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31600" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=-1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)}{{x}^{2}+3x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x+2]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -1}\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{x}{x+2})}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-1+2)+\\frac{-1}{-1+2})}{2(-1)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)-1}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c831ca73-a9fc-5de1-992d-91c98d87a7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264217} {:answer/id #uuid "37eebd4b-a06a-5c0a-9ecc-4d331b0b7425" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264218} {:answer/id #uuid "3f1a6353-435e-5444-b15f-67f6fb61a382" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264219} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecc71c5-0911-5a26-9042-66229f5633b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264220} {:answer/id #uuid "034da7b8-c843-5eab-b670-c8c206abff01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c831ca73-a9fc-5de1-992d-91c98d87a7c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264217} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0592163-4499-5d7e-aa23-9febdcecf081" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264216}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbac401d-8c8d-5591-9b6a-f972608b1dea" :question/math-academy-id "q-31602" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{-2(1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84c8628-5fbb-5d13-b29a-d6c444b39cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84c8628-5fbb-5d13-b29a-d6c444b39cf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264224} :answer-field/id #uuid "8636f221-317b-5f04-b70a-35d71fb0e0c0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264223}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8411c106-d2f4-578b-8564-df4ec0ed1b0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-336758" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})=-\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{-\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{3{x}^{-1}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90533308-3ecf-55cd-abff-87d11e9319a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264227}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90533308-3ecf-55cd-abff-87d11e9319a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264227} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f2985a-cac9-5db3-916b-e34e0ab57ca7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264226}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e36ca26f-ad8c-5e12-800b-7362e3a76a27" :question/math-academy-id "q-336798" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{ln\\,(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and  ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sec}⁡x]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\cancel{-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡x}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33f9c59a-257d-52f2-ab08-6986d283c94a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315d52c0-d1a2-5b6b-a646-e90d55b1759b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264230} {:answer/id #uuid "19ae7e7b-421c-5005-a291-10461c8552c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "315d52c0-d1a2-5b6b-a646-e90d55b1759b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264230} :answer-field/id #uuid "94a8f6a2-0cf7-5b7f-8cbb-88dc4ef14e43" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264229}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47c2f4a9-8658-5e42-99ca-a9c23bc8f50d" :question/math-academy-id "q-336883" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Note that plugging in $x=0$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{x}^{2}+3x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[{e}^{2x}-1]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}+3x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{2x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2(0)}}{2(0)+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{0+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264232 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d0a167-8e89-5c29-89cc-a5c9423a3c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264234} {:answer/id #uuid "3619c18b-55a3-5516-b3fa-6e2be40076ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45d0a167-8e89-5c29-89cc-a5c9423a3c3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264234} :answer-field/id #uuid "b869acb6-d4c6-57ea-a8b6-b4c495631520" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264233}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a1ad199e-1393-5a2a-997a-7d759f8f08bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-336901" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]}^{′}}{[16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2x-6}{(\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{x+1}}-4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{x}^{2}-6x+9}{16\\sqrt{x+1}-4x-20} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{x-3}{4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[x-3]}^{′}}{{[4(x+1)^{-1/2}-2]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{1}{-2(x+1)^{-3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to 3}(x+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(3+1)^{3/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04042993-3fef-5f55-95c5-115c760693bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\ln(9))^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264238} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46dd4f2-d324-5542-a7e1-3429ab75b92c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264237}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e0f7884-a830-5489-a8ed-a70ba5c64f35" :question/math-academy-id "q-336932" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-$\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}-{9}^{x}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)x}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{4}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-{9}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)]}^{′}}{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{4}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-{9}^{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}((\\operatorname{ln}⁡4)^{2}-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9)^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264239 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a305fab-740e-5e18-8aca-2acded269fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264241} {:answer/id #uuid "dfadd57c-05ac-533b-af96-66965a9b5919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a305fab-740e-5e18-8aca-2acded269fb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264241} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0d131b6-cfe1-593b-a2e0-c9068697be76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264240}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6bab4c0c-7677-579a-8ee7-52df23ea6308" :question/math-academy-id "q-336943" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{{[1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)]^{′}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4+2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a079fdeb-a9f0-5c4c-a139-1bac32966fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a079fdeb-a9f0-5c4c-a139-1bac32966fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264245} :answer-field/id #uuid "0296bdc1-e783-5066-818e-2de66b57a6b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264244}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a37eeab6-798e-5dc0-b5e5-c7c2a03e11e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-336953" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[5{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(10x+\\frac{1}{x})}{2} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit at Positive or Negative Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c64b3d3-ad22-5189-ad62-a6bd7048ff80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264248} {:answer/id #uuid "1c737534-f1a8-5f82-b27d-20b6e4aa2185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c737534-f1a8-5f82-b27d-20b6e4aa2185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264249} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2ed7e69-bc95-5a14-b35c-9f5ca8714996" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264247}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5abe0f2-94e8-57fc-a72a-431f152287b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-336966" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3/2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}+\\frac{1}{x}]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{-1/2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8f872b-6dc6-53e9-9375-a11573728d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264252} {:answer/id #uuid "d5572dd1-40b5-5472-96e5-b8731a9f950c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264253} {:answer/id #uuid "9697133b-54a1-5709-8bdc-407fdbdd8b53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264254} {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} {:answer/id #uuid "99e51ad0-94fb-5a17-a2b1-81e5fa5b77bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "032b7e57-209d-5e7f-97f2-53b407519105" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264255} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f36a378-4860-5a2e-8c9c-2f52d95b5aa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21b4d0f5-e835-5e6f-8545-1cd83c1350d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72333" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}{e}^{1/x}=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{1/x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]^{′}}{[{e}^{1/x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{{e}^{1/x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{{e}^{1/x}\\cdot {x}^{-2}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{x}{{e}^{1/x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f29435f-b112-5999-b0ca-9003d9e622e1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64cb2dc8-7204-5aca-bc9d-3cd91f16892c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264259} {:answer/id #uuid "58beefc3-031f-5cd5-a1cb-88d181ca8560" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264260} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1ebf43-45cc-5265-af34-b7beb51b50f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264261} {:answer/id #uuid "74263b53-7891-5221-9a88-5cdb3a71114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264262} {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d70b60a-66f3-5018-8388-0b5c424b7c01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264263} :answer-field/id #uuid "8317f879-b0d4-5e38-b202-437de1124e94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6b4e5b-14b3-521d-a29f-b6cecec742c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-72336" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}^{′}}{{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})}{(-\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{-3}}{4{x}^{-5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
